INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'taylor-swift-halloween-costume-backlash-ttpd-blocked-billie-20261101', 'taylor-swift', 'United States',
'Taylor Swift Halloween Costume Backlash: The ''TTPD Blocked Billie'' Joke Dividing Fans',
'',
'Taylor Swift Halloween costume backlash: a fan''s Billboard-chart Halloween outfit revived the 2024 ''TTPD blocked Billie'' row and split fans — here is what the costume showed, what really happened in 2024, and what to watch next.',
'',
$$<p>Taylor Swift Halloween costume backlash erupted on 30 October 2026, when a fan's Halloween outfit split Swift's own fandom in two. The costume, shared on TikTok, was a printed copy of the Billboard 200 chart showing The Tortured Poets Department at No. 1, Billie Eilish's HIT ME HARD AND SOFT at No. 2 and Charli XCX's BRAT at No. 3, captioned: "I love fangirl Halloween bc wdym you're 'ttpd blocked Billie'." What its creator appeared to mean as a joke, many other fans read as a celebration of the chart battles that have followed Swift since 2024.</p>
<h2>At a glance: Taylor Swift Halloween costume backlash</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Costume</th><td>A printed Billboard 200 chart, shared on TikTok ahead of Halloween</td></tr>
<tr><th>Chart shown</th><td>The Tortured Poets Department No. 1, HIT ME HARD AND SOFT No. 2, BRAT No. 3</td></tr>
<tr><th>Caption</th><td>"I love fangirl Halloween bc wdym you're 'ttpd blocked Billie'"</td></tr>
<tr><th>Reaction</th><td>Split: some fans called it a harmless joke, others called it degrading</td></tr>
<tr><th>Artists' response</th><td>No response from Swift, Eilish or Charli XCX is recorded in the report</td></tr>
<tr><th>Root of the row</th><td>The 2024 chart battles over TTPD, HIT ME HARD AND SOFT and BRAT</td></tr>
</table>
<h2>Taylor Swift Halloween costume backlash: what the outfit showed</h2>
<p><a href="https://www.indiatimes.com/trending/taylor-swift-fans-halloween-costume-referencing-billie-eilish-and-charli-xcx-chart-rivalry-sparks-backlash-swifties-are-the-reason-taylor-receives-hate/articleshow/135375413.html">India Times reported on 31 October 2026</a> that the row began after a TikTok user shared the chart costume, and that a screenshot of the post then began circulating on X. Users pointed out that the original poster appeared to be a Swiftie, with multiple posters of Swift visible in the background. That detail sharpened the argument: this was not an outsider mocking Swift, but a fan joke that other fans decided had gone too far.</p>
<p>The critics were blunt. One X user wrote, "fangirl yet you're contributing to degrade a woman because of charts". Another wrote, "I swear swifties are the reason for 99% of the hate taylor receives". Others argued the joke would rebound on Swift herself: "It just gives others more ammunition to hate Taylor. Especially when the whole 'block' narrative is people's biggest issue with her rn."</p>
<p>Not everyone agreed. Some fans viewed the costume as a playful pop-culture throwback. Fan backlashes over Swift are familiar territory, as covered in <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/taylor-swift-seahawks-end-game-friendship-bracelet-backlash-20261028">our report on the Seahawks friendship-bracelet backlash</a>: the argument here is less about the outfit than about what it celebrates.</p>
<h2>The 2024 chart battle behind the joke</h2>
<p>The costume only makes sense with the history behind it. Swift released The Tortured Poets Department on 19 April 2024. When Eilish released HIT ME HARD AND SOFT on 17 May 2024, both camps released version after version in the same tracking week. On the day Eilish's album came out, Swift released three limited digital editions with previously unheard "first-draft phone memo" bonus tracks. A "Fortnight" remix followed on 21 May 2024, and on 23 May 2024, the final day of the tracking week, three more digital versions arrived with live tracks from her Paris Eras Tour shows. Eilish answered with her own deluxe editions and a remix of "L'Amour de Ma Vie".</p>
<p>The result, on the Billboard 200 chart dated 1 June 2024, was a fifth consecutive week at No. 1 for The Tortured Poets Department, with 378,000 equivalent album units, according to Luminate data reported by Billboard. HIT ME HARD AND SOFT debuted at No. 2 with 339,000 units, a career best for Eilish, and the first week in eight years in which two albums each cleared 300,000 units.</p>
<p>That even-handed detail matters: the costume's shorthand compresses a two-sided release war into a one-sided accusation, and Swift's releases were never officially described as attempts to block Eilish.</p>
<h2>The BRAT chapter: why Charli XCX is on the costume too</h2>
<p>BRAT earned its place on the costume a month later. Released on 7 June 2024, it recorded the biggest opening week of Charli's career and led the midweek race for No. 1 on the UK Official Albums Chart. Then, on Thursday 13 June 2024, the final day of the UK tracking week, Taylor Nation announced six UK-exclusive digital deluxe variants of The Tortured Poets Department, available only until 11:59pm that evening. BRAT debuted at No. 2, while Swift's album took a sixth non-consecutive week at No. 1, becoming her longest-running UK No. 1, <a href="https://www.music-news.com/news/UK/174490/Charli-xcx-secures-her-biggest-opening-week-yet-with-BRAT">Official Charts reported</a>.</p>
<p>According to Taylor Nation, as reported by <a href="https://dcoasia.com/2024/06/15/taylor-swift-may-have-captured-the-charts-but-charli-xcx-captured-the-zeitgeist-the-guardian/">The Guardian</a>, the deluxe editions were released to celebrate Swift's Eras Tour arriving in the UK. Fans had another theory: that the UK-only variants were designed to hold Charli off the top spot. Swift later told New York magazine that she had been "blown away by Charli's melodic sensibilities since 2011". Neither artist has ever confirmed a chart-blocking intent, and the costume revives the accusation without settling it.</p>
<h2>Why the joke landed badly in October 2026</h2>
<p>Timing did the costume no favours. India Times reported that some Swifties allege Swift faces a coordinated smear campaign, while others say criticism of her should not be dismissed as one. In that climate, a joke built on the "blocked Billie" line reads differently depending on which side the viewer stands on. As covered in <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/taylor-swift-tim-mcgraw-wedding-performance-rumour-20261031">our report on Tim McGraw debunking the wedding performance rumour</a>, Swift's news cycle this week has already been full of corrections. The costume row is the fan-made addition: no new statement, no new release, just an old argument in a new outfit.</p>
<h2>What to watch next</h2>
<p>Three things are worth watching. The first is whether the costume debate grows across Halloween weekend or fades once the costumes come off. The second is whether Swift, Eilish or Charli XCX responds at all; none has commented on the costume in the reporting so far. The third is where fan attention goes next: Geo reported on 30 October 2026 that Swift is reportedly planning a public appearance at a Toy Story 5 screening in New York City on Sunday 1 November 2026, as part of the Oscar campaign for "I Knew It, I Knew You". That appearance is reported, not confirmed.</p>$$,
